The code confirms the bug. In `frame(ctx, state)`, when a collision is detected via `collides(state.ball, brick)`, the brick is set to `alive = false`, and score is updated, but the ball's velocity is NOT changed and the ball is NOT repositioned.

```javascript
    if (collides(state.ball, brick)) {
      brick.alive = false;
      state.combo += 1;
      state.score += scoreForBrick(brick, state.combo);
      // BUG (#1): the ball is NOT deflected here. Only the walls and the paddle
      // bounce (in step()); bricks were never given a bounce. So the ball plows
      // straight through a column head-on AND straight through the side of
// a row. Fix = a real per-face bounce (flip vy on a top/bottom hit, vx on a side hit)
// plus pushing the ball back out of the brick.
    }
```

This is a massive "naive" failure: it doesn't даже provide a bounce.

Now I will perform the search for state of the art (Step 1 of the playtest skill).
The symptom is "ball passes through bricks".
The mechanism is discrete collision detection of a circle (approximated as AABB) against a grid of AABBs.

Search queries:
1. "discrete collision detection tunneling swept AABB"
2. "AABB corner resolution circle"
3. "tile seam ghost collision"
4. "discrete collision detection multiple AABB bounce"

